 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<p>“ARTICLE I.</p>\n<p>There shall be perpetual peace and friendship between all the citizens of the United States of America, and all the individuals composing the whole Cherokee nation of Indians, . . .</p>\n<p>ARTICLE XIV.</p>\n<p>That the Cherokee nation may be led to a greater degree of civilization, and to become herdsmen and cultivators, instead of remaining in a state of hunters, the United States will from time to time furnish gratuitously the said nation with useful implements of husbandry, and further to assist the said nation in so desirable a pursuit, and at the same time to establish a certain mode of communication, the United States will send such, and so many persons to reside in said nation as they may judge proper, not exceeding four in number, who shall qualify themselves to act as interpreters. These persons shall have lands assigned by the Cherokees for cultivation for themselves and their successors in office; but they shall be precluded exercising any kind of traffic.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">The Treaty of Holston, July 2, 1791</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 615130,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Based on the excerpted treaty, the United States adopted a policy towards American Indians that it believed would",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168492,
     "content_html": "force American Indians off their land when the federal government deemed it necessary"
    },
    {
     "id": 1168491,
     "content_html": "allow American Indians to maintain their traditional lifestyle"
    },
    {
     "id": 1168493,
     "content_html": "bring progress and modernity to American Indian nations"
    },
    {
     "id": 1168490,
     "content_html": "provide independence and self-reliance to American Indian societies"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615131,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "How were the sentiments in the excerpt a continuation of English and American policy toward American Indians?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168496,
     "content_html": "The English and Americans wanted the land occupied by American Indians."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168497,
     "content_html": "The English and Americans believed their way of life superior to that of American Indians."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168495,
     "content_html": "The English and Americans wanted to change American Indians into wage laborers."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168494,
     "content_html": "The English and Americans wanted to maintain separate societies from those of the American Indians."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
